 [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "30 July –  3 August 1996"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/718"} "Lake Sidney Lanier, Gainesville, Georgia"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/256104"} "48 from 24 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Germany’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9894"} "Kay Bluhm"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9915"} "Torsten Gutsche"] " were the defending Olympic champions in the K-2 1000, as well as the 1993 World Champions and the 1995 runners-up. Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10207"} "Daniele Scarpa"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10201"} "Antonio Rossi"] ", meanwhile, were the 1995 World Champions and the 1993 and 1994 runners-up. These two nations were the favorites in the event at the 1996 Summer Olympics and were joined in winning their heats in the opening round by Australia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9021"} "Grant Leury"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9035"} "Peter Scott"] ". Italy won the first heat in the semi-finals, nearly eliminating Leury and Scott in the process, who survived only by being the fastest of the fifth place finishers. The Germans, meanwhile, were bested by both " [:a {:href "/athletes/10467"} "Dariusz Białkowski"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10494"} "Grzegorz Kotowicz"] " of Poland, the 1995 World bronze medalists, and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9646"} "Patrick Lancereau"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9655"} "Pierre Lubac"] " of France. The Italians were dominant in the medal round, winning gold by nearly a second and a half over the Germans, who settled for silver. The race for the final podium spot, meanwhile, turned into a three-way battle between Bulgarians " [:a {:href "/athletes/9196"} "Andrian Dushev"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9205"} "Milko Kazanov"] ", Poland, and France, who finished in that order 0.196 apart. Although Bluhm and Gutsche failed to defend their crown, they would have their revenge against Scarpa in the K-2 500, where they took gold over Italy by a mere 0.032 seconds. Rossi, meanwhile, would go on to become a double Olympic champion by winning the K-1 500 the following day."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Rep"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "184/185"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/10201"} "Antonio Rossi"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/10207"} "Daniele Scarpa"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ITA.png"}] "ITA"]] [:td {} "3:33.786 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "3:16.848 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "3:09.190 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "132/141"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/9894"} "Kay Bluhm"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/9915"} "Torsten Gutsche"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {} "3:39.388 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "3:19.394 (3 h2)"] [:td {} "3:10.518 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "042/041"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/9196"} "Andrian Dushev"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/9205"} "Milko Kazanov"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/BUL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/BUL.png"}] "BUL"]] [:td {} "3:39.914 (3 h1)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "3:18.480 (3 h1)"] [:td {} "3:11.206 (3)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:t